 Be sure to commit the `uv.lock` and `pyproject.toml` files with `uv lock` to reflect any changes in dependencies.
 
+# CPU inference with MNN
+
+The `-mnn` image (or `uv sync --extra mnn` plus `MACHINE_LEARNING_MNN=true`) runs the facial recognition models (and, with `MACHINE_LEARNING_MNN_TEXTUAL=true`, the CLIP text tower) on [MNN](https://github.com/alibaba/MNN)'s CPU backend instead of ONNX Runtime. Models are converted from the ONNX files on first load and cached next to them (`model.mnn` with fp16 weights, plus a `model.mnn.json` with the ONNX input/output signature); models that fail to convert fall back to ONNX Runtime. The arithmetic runs in fp32 by default (`MACHINE_LEARNING_MNN_PRECISION`), which is exact and, on Arm CPUs, 2-3x faster than ONNX Runtime for the face detector and recogniser at a fraction of the memory. fp16 arithmetic is faster still but MNN 3.6 returned wrong outputs for some inputs of the ArcFace and CLIP text models with it, so validate before enabling. CLIP image models and OCR stay on ONNX Runtime (or the ggml backend).
+
 # Load Testing
 
 To measure inference throughput and latency, you can use [Locust](https://locust.io/) using the provided `locustfile.py`.
